  "date": "2026-10-26",
  "text": "Shorting the Nasdaq at 14:30.\n\nIf this is your strategy, your next trade may as well be with your risk manager at the casino.\nIt’s the trading version of wanting results overnight. And quick wins aren’t a strategy, in markets or in life.\n\nI’ve been taught that trading isn’t “financial gambling.” In week 1, I understood that in theory. Putting it into practice has been a different lesson.\n\nHere is some of what I’ve learned so far.\n\nTrading is two disciplines working together.\nFundamental analysis: understanding the economic data, policy decisions and news that shape where a market is heading.\nTechnical analysis: reading price and market structure to decide when to act, and when not to.\nWhen both agree, there’s a case for a trade. When they don’t, the best trade is no trade.\n\nThe harder part has been learning about myself. Reviewing my own decisions, I’ve noticed patterns that have names for a reason:\nLoss aversion: holding onto a losing position instead of accepting the loss.\nAuthority bias: following a decision because someone more experienced made it.\nAction bias: feeling I should be doing something when the right move was to wait.\n\nKnowing these biases exist hasn’t made me immune to them. Building a process has helped:\nA clear plan before every decision.\nRisk defined upfront.\nSet limits, and time to step back after a setback.\n\nAt the end of the day, it’s about behaviours. Whether you’re up or down, the process stays the same.\nMany thanks to Mark Boardman DipPFS and Robert Russell for their continued guidance, and for holding us to a standard where process comes before outcome.\n\nZISHI"
